---
import Docs from '../../layouts/Docs.astro';
import { href } from '../../i18n/routes';

const lang = 'en' as const;
const page = 'labels' as const;
---
<Docs lang={lang} page={page}
  title="Labels & decorations"
  description="The nineteen labels and their three kinds, what the toolkit adds to Conventional Comments, decorations, blocking precedence rules, and the sixteen diagnostics.">
<div class="dhead stack g12">
          <p class="crumb"><b>docs</b> / labels &amp; decorations</p>
          <h1>Labels &amp; decorations</h1>
          <p class="lede measure">Nineteen labels in three kinds, three built-in decorations, and five precedence rules determine how a comment is read — including whether its thread counts as blocking. Twelve of the labels come from Conventional Comments; seven are added by the toolkit, and this page says which. It is the reference for all of them.</p>
        </div>

        <section class="dsec">
          <h2>The grammar</h2>
          <p>Every validated comment starts with a prefix line. Anything after the first blank line is discussion. It is left entirely untouched by the parser.</p>
          <div class="anno">
            <pre class="line"><span class="tk-l">label</span> <span class="tk-d">[decorations]</span><span class="tk-c">:</span> subject

<span class="tk-k">[discussion]</span></pre>
            <div class="legend">
              <i style="color:var(--accent)">■ label — required, lowercase</i>
              <i>■ decorations — optional, comma-separated, inside parentheses</i>
              <i style="color:var(--block)">■ : — separates the prefix from the subject</i>
            </div>
          </div>
          <p class="small">Casing errors are corrected rather than rejected. For example, <code>Issue:</code> raises <code>W-CASE</code>, a warning with an automatic fix.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="dsec">
          <h2>Nineteen labels, three kinds</h2>
          <p>A review puts two roles face to face, and Conventional Comments does not tell them apart: the <strong>reviewer</strong>, who reads the pull request and raises points, and <strong>the people who carry it</strong> — its author and its maintainers — who answer them. Every label has a <strong>kind</strong>, which says what the label does with a point:</p>
          <ul class="bul">
            <li><strong>A remark raises a point.</strong> It is first the reviewer's gesture, and the case of every label Conventional Comments defines.</li>
            <li><strong>A reply answers a remark.</strong> It is first the gesture of the people who carry the pull request.</li>
            <li><strong>A status label does neither.</strong> It says where the review itself stands: one pass done, or the whole review closed.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>The kind never changes a verdict: not what is valid, not what blocks, not whether a thread is resolved. It does two things. It decides where the button sits in the toolbar — remarks first, then status labels, which close the first row; replies always on a row of their own. And it keeps replies and status labels apart from remarks when label usage is counted, so that a thank-you or a recap never dilutes the share of <code>praise</code>. Nothing is reserved for anyone: every label is offered to everyone, and an author may perfectly well answer a <code>question</code> with a <code>note</code>.</p>
          <p>The table follows the order of the toolbar. Every label ships enabled, with one exception: <code>quibble</code>, which Conventional Comments itself describes as a <code>nitpick</code> under another name. A repository that wants it enables it with one entry in its configuration.</p>
          <div class="scroller">
            <table class="t">
              <thead>
                <tr><th>Label</th><th>What it means</th><th>Blocking</th><th>Shortcut</th></tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody data-kind="remark">
                <tr class="grp"><th colspan="4" scope="rowgroup"><div class="gh"><span class="gk">Remarks</span><span class="gd">raise a point</span><span class="go">Conventional Comments · 12</span></div></th></tr>
                <tr><td class="m"><span class="pill" style="--lc:var(--l-praise)">👏 praise</span></td><td class="d">Highlights something done well.</td><td class="m d">Depends on decoration</td><td class="m">?pr</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="m"><span class="pill" style="--lc:var(--l-nitpick)">🔍 nitpick</span></td><td class="d">A minor preference with nothing significant at stake.</td><td class="m"><span class="deco nb">Never</span></td><td class="m">?ni</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="m"><span class="pill" style="--lc:var(--l-suggestion)">💡 suggestion</span></td><td class="d">A reasoned proposal for improving something.</td><td class="m d">Depends on decoration</td><td class="m">?su</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="m"><span class="pill" style="--lc:var(--l-issue)">🔨 issue</span></td><td class="d">Identifies a problem. Ideally accompanied by a suggestion.</td><td class="m"><span class="deco b">By default</span></td><td class="m">?i · ?ib</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="m"><span class="pill" style="--lc:var(--l-todo)">📌 todo</span></td><td class="d">A small but necessary change.</td><td class="m"><span class="deco b">By default</span></td><td class="m">?to</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="m"><span class="pill" style="--lc:var(--l-question)">❓ question</span></td><td class="d">Asks for clarification about something uncertain.</td><td class="m d">Depends on decoration</td><td class="m">?que</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="m"><span class="pill" style="--lc:var(--l-thought)">💭 thought</span></td><td class="d">An idea that came up during the review, with no action requested.</td><td class="m"><span class="deco nb">Never</span></td><td class="m">?th</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="m"><span class="pill" style="--lc:var(--l-chore)">🧹 chore</span></td><td class="d">A side task that should be completed before acceptance.</td><td class="m"><span class="deco b">By default</span></td><td class="m">?ch</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="m"><span class="pill" style="--lc:var(--l-note)">📝 note</span></td><td class="d">Information worth calling out.</td><td class="m"><span class="deco nb">Never</span></td><td class="m">?no</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="m"><span class="pill" style="--lc:var(--l-typo)">🔤 typo</span></td><td class="d">A typing mistake.</td><td class="m d">Depends on decoration</td><td class="m">?ty</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="m"><span class="pill" style="--lc:var(--l-polish)">✨ polish</span></td><td class="d">A finishing improvement.</td><td class="m d">Depends on decoration</td><td class="m">?po</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="m" style="opacity:.6"><span class="pill" style="--lc:var(--l-quibble)">🪶 quibble</span></td><td class="d">A debatable detail.</td><td class="m d">Disabled by default</td><td class="m">?qui</td></tr>
              </tbody>
              <tbody class="added" data-kind="status">
                <tr class="grp"><th colspan="4" scope="rowgroup"><div class="gh"><span class="gk">Review status</span><span class="gd">say where the review stands</span><span class="go">added by the toolkit · 2</span></div></th></tr>
                <tr><td class="m"><span class="pill" style="--lc:var(--l-summary)">📋 summary</span></td><td class="d">Recaps the review, or one pass of it: what was checked, what is still open.</td><td class="m"><span class="deco nb">Never</span></td><td class="m">?sum</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="m"><span class="pill" style="--lc:var(--l-reviewed)">🎉 reviewed</span></td><td class="d">Closes your own review: your points are dealt with, you have nothing to add. Approves nothing.</td><td class="m"><span class="deco nb">Never</span></td><td class="m">?r</td></tr>
              </tbody>
              <tbody class="added" data-kind="reply">
                <tr class="grp"><th colspan="4" scope="rowgroup"><div class="gh"><span class="gk">Replies</span><span class="gd">answer a remark</span><span class="go">added by the toolkit · 5</span></div></th></tr>
                <tr><td class="m"><span class="pill" style="--lc:var(--l-thanks)">🤝 thanks</span></td><td class="d">Says thank you, without taking a position on the point or on the code.</td><td class="m"><span class="deco nb">Never</span></td><td class="m">?thx</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="m"><span class="pill" style="--lc:var(--l-done)">✅ done</span></td><td class="d">The code changed because of the remark.</td><td class="m"><span class="deco nb">Never</span></td><td class="m">?do</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="m"><span class="pill" style="--lc:var(--l-ack)">👌 ack</span></td><td class="d">Received and accepted; the code has not changed.</td><td class="m"><span class="deco nb">Never</span></td><td class="m">?a</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="m"><span class="pill" style="--lc:var(--l-decision)">⚖️ decision</span></td><td class="d">Records the choice <em>not</em> to address a remark, and says why.</td><td class="m"><span class="deco nb">Never</span></td><td class="m">?de</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="m"><span class="pill" style="--lc:var(--l-disagree)">✋ disagree</span></td><td class="d">Declines the remark, and says why. Carries no authority and resolves nothing.</td><td class="m"><span class="deco nb">Never</span></td><td class="m">?di</td></tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
          <p class="small">Colours and icons are deliberately cosmetic. They are excluded from the enterprise floor. Changing them can never change a verdict or be locked down by an administrator.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="dsec">
          <h2>What the toolkit adds to Conventional Comments</h2>
          <p>Seven of these nineteen labels are <strong>not part of Conventional Comments</strong>: <code>summary</code>, <code>reviewed</code>, <code>thanks</code>, <code>done</code>, <code>ack</code>, <code>decision</code> and <code>disagree</code>. In the table above, they are the two groups marked “added by the toolkit”.</p>
          <p>The convention gives a vocabulary to the remark, and says nothing about what comes after it: how to answer one, or how to say that a review is over. It also invites teams to go beyond its list — <em>“Feel free to diverge from this specific list of labels if it seems appropriate.”</em> The toolkit does exactly that, on ground the convention does not cover. These labels are an addition of this tool, they claim no other standing, and a team can remove them.</p>
          <div class="callout">
            <span class="ct">to stick to the upstream list</span>
            <h4>Disable them, one entry each</h4>
            <p>There is no separate switch: a label is turned off with <code>enabled</code>, like any other. The extension's options page shows this block and copies it for you.</p>
          </div>
          <pre class="code"><span class="tk-k">&#123;</span>
  <span class="tk-k">"labels"</span>: [
    <span class="tk-k">&#123;</span> <span class="tk-k">"id"</span>: <span class="tk-v">"summary"</span>,  <span class="tk-k">"enabled"</span>: <span class="tk-v">false</span> <span class="tk-k">&#125;</span>,
    <span class="tk-k">&#123;</span> <span class="tk-k">"id"</span>: <span class="tk-v">"reviewed"</span>, <span class="tk-k">"enabled"</span>: <span class="tk-v">false</span> <span class="tk-k">&#125;</span>,
    <span class="tk-k">&#123;</span> <span class="tk-k">"id"</span>: <span class="tk-v">"thanks"</span>,   <span class="tk-k">"enabled"</span>: <span class="tk-v">false</span> <span class="tk-k">&#125;</span>,
    <span class="tk-k">&#123;</span> <span class="tk-k">"id"</span>: <span class="tk-v">"done"</span>,     <span class="tk-k">"enabled"</span>: <span class="tk-v">false</span> <span class="tk-k">&#125;</span>,
    <span class="tk-k">&#123;</span> <span class="tk-k">"id"</span>: <span class="tk-v">"ack"</span>,      <span class="tk-k">"enabled"</span>: <span class="tk-v">false</span> <span class="tk-k">&#125;</span>,
    <span class="tk-k">&#123;</span> <span class="tk-k">"id"</span>: <span class="tk-v">"disagree"</span>, <span class="tk-k">"enabled"</span>: <span class="tk-v">false</span> <span class="tk-k">&#125;</span>
  ]
<span class="tk-k">&#125;</span></pre>
          <p class="small"><code>decision</code> is deliberately not in that block. It belongs to the toolkit too, but it carries a rule of its own, described below, and is turned off separately.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="dsec">
          <h2>Replying to a remark</h2>
          <p>A repository can ask for <em>every</em> comment of a pull request to follow the convention, replies included (<code>scope.validateReplies</code>). Without labels made for replying, that meant writing <code>note: fixed</code> or <code>thought: good catch</code> — bending labels away from what they mean. Four labels fill that gap, and nothing else: <code>thanks</code>, <code>done</code>, <code>ack</code> and <code>disagree</code>. None of them can ever block.</p>
          <pre class="code"><span class="tk-l">issue</span> <span class="tk-d">(blocking)</span><span class="tk-c">:</span> platform CSS tokens leak into the shared stylesheet

<span class="tk-cm">— and, in the same thread, the author's reply —</span>

<span class="tk-l">done</span><span class="tk-c">:</span> removed the platform-specific token</pre>
          <p><code>done</code> says the code changed, so there is a diff to read again; <code>ack</code> says the remark is accepted and the code did not change. Three of the four accept a second spelling: <code>thx</code> for <code>thanks</code>, <code>fixed</code> for <code>done</code>, <code>acknowledged</code> for <code>ack</code>.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="dsec">
          <h2><code style="font-size:.9em">decision</code> — setting a remark aside, and saying why</h2>
          <p><code>decision</code> is a reply too, with one more requirement. It records the choice <em>not</em> to address a remark, and it has to explain that choice. It is written by the people who carry the pull request, in the thread it answers. It documents the choice; it does not resolve the thread, which remains a gesture of the platform.</p>
          <div class="split">
            <div class="callout">
              <span class="ct">it is recognized even where replies are not normally validated</span>
              <h4>The one label detected in an otherwise unvalidated reply</h4>
              <p>Thread replies are not validated by default (<code>validateReplies: false</code>). A reply whose resolved label is <code>decision</code> is still recognized. Otherwise, its rule would only apply in repositories that had independently enabled validation for replies.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="callout">
              <span class="ct">and a decision must actually explain the decision</span>
              <h4><code>E-DECISION-SUBJECT</code> is an error, not a warning</h4>
              <p>A <code>decision</code> reply must meet <code>minDecisionSubjectLength</code>, which defaults to 20 characters. That is the only subject-length threshold applied to <code>decision</code>. Setting a remark aside therefore requires an actual explanation. <code>wontfix</code> is not enough.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <pre class="code"><span class="tk-l">decision</span><span class="tk-c">:</span> out of scope for this PR, debt tracked in PROJ-142

Agreed with the platform team on 3 Sep: the cache rewrite lands in Q4
and this handler is deleted with it.</pre>
          <div class="callout">
            <span class="ct">not to be confused</span>
            <h4><code>disagree</code> is not <code>decision</code></h4>
            <p><code>disagree</code> is an opinion: anyone can voice it, and it settles nothing. <code>decision</code> records a choice made by the people who carry the pull request. The toolkit checks no authority behind either of them, and neither resolves the thread.</p>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="dsec">
          <h2>Saying where the review stands</h2>
          <p>Two gestures come up in every review and had no label: recapping a pass, and saying you are done. <code>summary</code> says what was checked and what is still open. <code>reviewed</code> closes your own review: each of your points is dealt with, and you have nothing to add. They are counterparts — <code>summary</code> describes a review that continues, <code>reviewed</code> one that is closed.</p>
          <pre class="code"><span class="tk-l">summary</span><span class="tk-c">:</span> second pass — the cache fix is verified, the naming point is still open

<span class="tk-cm">— later, once that last point is settled —</span>

<span class="tk-l">reviewed</span><span class="tk-c">:</span> nothing left on my side</pre>
          <div class="split">
            <div class="callout">
              <span class="ct">when to post it</span>
              <h4><code>reviewed</code> closes a review, not a reading pass</h4>
              <p>Post it once each of your points is dealt with — fixed and checked, or set aside by a reply you accept — and a last reading raises nothing new. A later change to the pull request reopens your review: the new changes have to be read before another <code>reviewed</code>.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="callout">
              <span class="ct">what neither of them does</span>
              <h4>They approve nothing</h4>
              <p>Approval belongs to the platform — <em>Approve</em> on GitHub, the vote on Azure DevOps — and the toolkit does not count <code>reviewed</code> comments. A <code>summary</code> never stands in for <code>reviewed</code>, even when no point is left open; posted after your <code>reviewed</code>, it reads as if your review had reopened.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <p class="small">Review agents publish a <code>summary</code> at each pass. See <a href={href('aireview', 'en')}>AI-assisted review</a>.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="dsec">
          <h2>Decorations</h2>
          <p>Decorations qualify the meaning of a label. Three built-in decorations affect the blocking verdict. Custom decorations are also allowed by default: they can carry useful meaning for people without influencing whether the comment blocks.</p>
          <div class="scroller">
            <table class="t">
              <thead><tr><th>Decoration</th><th>Effect</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr><td class="m"><span class="deco b">blocking</span></td><td class="m" style="color:var(--block)">Blocking</td><td class="d">Must be resolved before the subject under review can be accepted.</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="m"><span class="deco nb">non-blocking</span></td><td class="m" style="color:var(--pass)">Non-blocking</td><td class="d">Must not prevent acceptance.</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="m"><span class="deco nb">if-minor</span></td><td class="m" style="color:var(--pass)">Non-blocking</td><td class="d">Address it only if the required change turns out to be trivial. The author decides.</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="m"><span class="deco">security</span> <span class="deco">test</span> <span class="deco">ux</span></td><td class="m d">None</td><td class="d">Custom decorations. They can be displayed, counted, and indexed, but never affect the verdict. Organizations can define their own.</td></tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
          <p class="small">An undecorated <code>suggestion</code> or <code>question</code> raises <code>W-MISSING-DECORATION</code>. Those are the two labels for which a reader genuinely cannot infer the blocking intent from the label alone.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="dsec">
          <h2>Precedence — how blocking is determined</h2>
          <p>Five rules are evaluated in order. The first matching rule determines the result. There are no additional heuristics or hidden inputs.</p>
          <div class="scroller">
            <table class="t">
              <thead><tr><th>#</th><th>Condition</th><th>Result</th><th>Diagnostic</th></tr></thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr><td class="m num">1</td><td class="d">The label is <code>alwaysNonBlocking</code>, but a decoration forces blocking.</td><td class="m">Use the label's own default.</td><td class="m" style="color:var(--block)">E-CONFLICT</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="m num">2</td><td class="d">One decoration forces blocking while another forces non-blocking.</td><td class="m">Use the label's own default.</td><td class="m" style="color:var(--block)">E-CONFLICT</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="m num">3</td><td class="d">A decoration forces blocking.</td><td class="m" style="color:var(--block)">Blocking</td><td class="m d">—</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="m num">4</td><td class="d">A decoration forces non-blocking.</td><td class="m" style="color:var(--pass)">Non-blocking</td><td class="m d">—</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="m num">5</td><td class="d">No decoration forces either result.</td><td class="m">Use the label's <code>blockingByDefault</code> value.</td><td class="m d">—</td></tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
          <div class="callout">
            <span class="ct">why a conflicting comment still gets a verdict</span>
            <p style="margin:0">Rules 1 and 2 report an error, but they still produce a result. When a comment contradicts itself, the label's default is used as a deterministic fallback. Leaving the blocking state undefined would be worse: neither the banner nor a reader could tell whether the thread counts as blocking, and nothing in the comment would say how to settle it.</p>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="dsec">
          <h2>Diagnostics</h2>
          <p>There are sixteen diagnostic codes. <strong>Errors</strong> make a comment non-compliant: in <code>enforce</code> mode, the extension refuses to send it. <strong>Warnings</strong> are reported, and never cause a refusal by themselves. A repository can give each diagnostic another severity through <code>severities</code>, within two limits: an error can be lowered to a warning but never silenced, and <code>W-SESSION-MULTIPLE</code> can never be raised to an error.</p>
          <div class="split">
            <div class="scroller">
              <table class="t">
                <thead><tr><th style="color:var(--block)">Diagnostic</th><th>Raised when</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr><td class="m">E-NO-LABEL</td><td class="d">The comment has no prefix line.</td></tr>
                  <tr><td class="m">E-MALFORMED-PREFIX</td><td class="d">The prefix resembles valid syntax but cannot be parsed.</td></tr>
                  <tr><td class="m">E-UNKNOWN-LABEL</td><td class="d">The label is not enabled for this repository.</td></tr>
                  <tr><td class="m">E-EMPTY-SUBJECT</td><td class="d">A label and colon are present, but the subject is empty.</td></tr>
                  <tr><td class="m">E-DECORATION-SYNTAX</td><td class="d">Decoration parentheses or separators are malformed.</td></tr>
                  <tr><td class="m">E-UNKNOWN-DECORATION</td><td class="d">The decoration is unknown and custom decorations are disabled.</td></tr>
                  <tr><td class="m">E-CONFLICT</td><td class="d">Blocking precedence rule 1 or 2 applies.</td></tr>
                  <tr><td class="m">E-DECISION-SUBJECT</td><td class="d">A <code>decision</code> does not provide a sufficient explanation.</td></tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
            <div class="scroller">
              <table class="t">
                <thead><tr><th style="color:var(--warn)">Diagnostic</th><th>Raised when</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr><td class="m">W-CASE</td><td class="d">The label uses the wrong casing. Includes an automatic fix.</td></tr>
                  <tr><td class="m">W-DECORATION-STYLE</td><td class="d">Decoration spacing or ordering is inconsistent. Includes an automatic fix.</td></tr>
                  <tr><td class="m">W-SUBJECT-TOO-SHORT</td><td class="d">The subject contains fewer than 5 characters.</td></tr>
                  <tr><td class="m">W-SUBJECT-TOO-LONG</td><td class="d">The subject exceeds 120 characters.</td></tr>
                  <tr><td class="m">W-MISSING-DECORATION</td><td class="d">A <code>suggestion</code> or <code>question</code> has no decoration.</td></tr>
                  <tr><td class="m">W-NO-DISCUSSION</td><td class="d">A blocking comment contains no discussion.</td></tr>
                  <tr><td class="m">W-NOT-BLOCKABLE</td><td class="d">A comment is marked as blocking in a context that cannot carry blocking state.</td></tr>
                  <tr><td class="m">W-SESSION-MULTIPLE</td><td class="d">A comment carries more than one <a href={href('aireview', 'en')}>session marker</a>.</td></tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="callout acc">
            <span class="ct">calibration matters</span>
            <h4>A short subject is deliberately only a warning.</h4>
            <p>The threshold is five characters, and falling below it never produces an error. A short subject is not serious enough to justify blocking a workflow. Making minor issues punitive encourages people to work around the convention instead of using it. Enforcement works better when it reserves hard failures for things that genuinely matter.</p>
          </div>
        </section>

        <div class="pager">
          <a class="pgl" href={href('howitworks', 'en')}><span class="dir">← previous</span><span class="ttl">How it works</span></a>
          <a class="pgl" href={href('adoption', 'en')} style="text-align:right"><span class="dir">next →</span><span class="ttl">Adoption</span></a>
        </div>
</Docs>
